  "evidence": [
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:research-umd-starch-gelatinization-thickening","source_ref":"source:research-umd-starch-hydrolysis-amylase","anchor":{"section":"Introduction, paragraph beginning ‘When an aqueous suspension of starch is heated’"},"note":"The source states that heating aqueous starch weakens hydrogen bonds, water is absorbed, granules swell, and gelatinization produces a gelatinous, highly viscous consistency used to thicken broth. No dish, amount, cooking time, measured viscosity, or completed result is established.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:research-vt-vegetable-fermentation-submersion-salt-temperature","source_ref":"source:research-vt-vegetable-fermentation","anchor":{"section":"What is fermentation?; Fermentation Components; Temperature; Weights"},"note":"The source states that brining or salting and atmosphere control favor fermenters, that submerged brine keeps oxygen away and gives lactic bacteria a competitive advantage, and that salt amount and temperature affect fermenting populations. No recipe, measured ratio, vessel, timetable, safety verdict, or outcome for a batch is established.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:research-uf-fish-heating-protein-water-texture","source_ref":"source:research-uf-ifas-fish-fillet-structure","anchor":{"section":"Why do the color and texture of the fish fillet change after cooking?"},"note":"UF/IFAS states that heating fish denatures muscle proteins, reduces water-binding capacity and shrinks muscle; heated flesh becomes opaque and firm, while collagen changes contribute to flaking. No temperature threshold, doneness, safety result, species-specific texture, or concrete fish is established.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:research-fao-fish-gutting-gall-bladder","source_ref":"source:research-fao-preliminary-freshwater-fish-processing","anchor":{"section":"3.3.7 Gutting"},"note":"FAO describes gutting as removal of internal organs and optional cleaning of blood and cavity tissues, and directs special care to avoid cutting the gall bladder. No claim that damage occurred, that washing repairs it, or that a fish is clean, edible, or spoiled is established.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:research-fao-exposed-fish-drying-pests","source_ref":"source:research-fao-fish-and-fish-products","anchor":{"section":"Dried fish"},"note":"FAO states that sun heat and moving air remove moisture, while traditional open sun-drying is weather-dependent and exposes fish to insects, vermin, sand and dirt. No local weather, drying duration, contamination event, preservation endpoint, or storage result is established.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:research-fao-fish-salting-diffusion","source_ref":"source:research-fao-fish-and-fish-products","anchor":{"section":"Salted fish"},"note":"FAO states that salting or brining moves water from fish flesh to the external solution and salt into the flesh; effectiveness depends on salt distribution, concentration, time and whether drying is combined. No dosage, preservation outcome, or safe storage duration is established.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:research-msu-cold-storage-not-sterilization","source_ref":"source:research-msu-cooling-refrigerating-food","anchor":{"section":"Cooling and Refrigerating Food, bullets beginning ‘Refrigeration does not stop bacteria’ and ‘Freezing DOES NOT kill bacteria’"},"note":"The source says refrigeration slows but does not stop bacterial growth and freezing does not kill bacteria. This does not establish any storage method, temperature, duration, microbial load, food safety, or outcome for a particular item.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:research-cdc-rodent-attraction-and-exclusion","source_ref":"source:research-cdc-seal-up-rodents","anchor":{"section":"Seal up gaps and holes; Seal up food and water sources inside"},"note":"CDC states that rodents are attracted to food, water and nesting sites, and recommends sealing openings and keeping food enclosed. Modern materials are examples, not imported technology; no infestation, species, barrier performance, contamination, or scene outcome is established.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:research-osu-honeybee-forage-weather","source_ref":"source:research-osu-honeybee-colony-pollination","anchor":{"section":"Honey bee flight; Colony size and efficiency; Food requirement"},"note":"OSU states that honey-bee foraging needs suitable light and temperature, poor weather can interrupt collection, and surplus honey depends on forager number and forage availability. No local weather, hive, floral patch, honey amount, harvest, or yield is established.","review_status":"approved"}
  ],
